//BUG="a destructuring assignment is only taken apart one level deep"
//OUT="1 2 3"

// `lower_statement` (src/compiler/lowerer/mod.rs) rewrites an assignment
// whose left side is a tuple or struct literal into
//
//     $tmp = <value>
//     (a, b) = $tmp.0
//     c = $tmp.1
//
// via `gen_struct_destructuring`. The statements it generates are returned
// as they are — `lower_statements` only flattens them into the block — so
// the first of them, whose left side is again a tuple, is never
// destructured in turn. The normalizer then treats `(a, b)` as a value to be
// built from the existing variables `a` and `b`, and the compile fails with
// "Variable with name a not found."
//
// Nested tuples are otherwise supported as values and as types
// (09_tuples/13_parentheses_nest_a_tuple.cpl), and a pattern that mirrors
// the value is the obvious way to take one apart — a pair of coordinates
// and a weight, say.
//
// The fix is to apply the destructuring rewrite to the assignments it
// generates, without lowering their already-lowered right-hand sides again.

fn main
    (a, b), c = (1, 2), 3
    out "{a} {b} {c}"
